  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1157 AM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nAs of noon CST, temperatures across the area are in the mid 30s \nwith winds out of the northeast to east across central SD and out \nof the north across northeast SD. Fog that was present this \nmorning has burned off but a lower level stratus layer remains in \nplace. These clouds are expected to continue into the afternoon \nand begin clearing later this evening.\n\nCool high pressure continues to build into the area today and will \nremain in place through the day Friday. Today will be the coolest of \nthe week as temperatures will increase by a few degrees each day \ngoing into the weekend. Next week will be much warmer with highs in \nthe 50s to perhaps low 60s in south central SD. Low pressure forms \nover the northern Rockies, just north of the MT/Canada border Friday \nand a pressure gradient sets up across western and central SD. This \nwill result in breezy conditions for west river counties Friday \nafternoon with gusts of 30-35 mph expected. This will continue \nSaturday and spread east into the remainder of central SD with a 30-\n50% chance of wind gusts of 35 mph or higher Saturday afternoon. \nCurrent forecast has some wind gusts to around 40 mph. Winds \ngenerally in that 30-40 mph range will continue across different \nareas of the CWA through at least Tuesday. The low from the northern \nRockies will move into the western Dakotas Tuesday with a cold front \ncrossing the CWA Wednesday. Models are showing a chance of \nprecipitation with this frontal passage.",
